I've written the plan for Day 268: two task files plus issue_responses.md in session_plan/. The commit command didn't record them because .gitignore excludes session_plan/, so the files exist only on disk. I didn't force-add them, on the assumption that the harness reads that directory directly.

Task 1 — Fixes #1231 (product). Running with -p currently turns on --yes automatically. So a command the safety check warns about, like rm -rf /home, runs without asking anyone. The plan sets one rule per way of starting yoyo:

| How yoyo was started | Warned command |
|---|---|
| Interactive REPL | asks (unchanged) |
| --yes typed explicitly | runs (unchanged; #1202's decision stands for these users) |
| -p without --yes | refused, with the reason and the fix ("rerun with --yes, or add an allow rule") |
| piped input, no -p or --yes | measured and written down, not changed |

The rule lives in one small function with a test per row. Before any code changes, the implementer has to:
• search scripts/ and .github/ for loop runs that use -p;
• stop and ask @yuanhao if a file I'm not allowed to edit depends on today's behaviour;
• post the findings on #1231.

The task also covers:
• a check that the new tests fail when the refusal is disabled, run below the level where anything actually gets deleted;
• the per-mode table in docs/src/troubleshooting/safety.md;
• a comment on #1202. Its stated reason, "Claude Code's bypass mode is the same", holds for an explicit --yes but not for plain -p.

Task 2 — Fixes #1230 (product; this is the session's backlog-closing slot). This is a false alarm I introduced last night. rm -rf "$PWD/target" asks for confirmation, warning that $PWD might be empty, but the shell always sets it to the working folder. The fix only stops warning about $PWD when a real path follows it. These all still get flagged:
• bare $PWD, "$PWD" and $PWD/;
• look-alike variables such as $OLDPWD and $PWDX;
• a second unknown variable in the same path;
• commands that unset $PWD first.

Because this narrows a safety rule, the implementer compares the old and new check over a generated set of spellings and lists every case that changes. One case to watch: "$PWD/.." (the parent folder) must still be flagged. If it isn't flagged today either, it becomes its own issue.

File size limits. src/safety.rs is exactly at its line cap. src/tools.rs (5301 vs 5286) and src/cli.rs (7885 vs 7861) are both a little over their recorded sizes but still inside the allowed margin. Both tasks say how to stay under the caps or update the cap list with a reason.

